-- js-controlflow.rofl — THE THIRD LAYER, and it exists to answer a question the
-- call graph cannot: not WHICH function a site reaches, but WHETHER the site
-- runs at all.
--
-- WHY THIS LAYER EARNS ITS CELLS. The call-graph acceptance measures the model
-- against a running program and reports two error directions: an edge the
-- runtime took and the model missed (UNSOUND), and an edge the model derived
-- and the runtime never took (over-approximation). The second list has been a
-- flat one, and it conflates two completely different things — an edge the
-- oracle CANNOT SEE because of how V8 names frames, and an edge that really was
-- not taken because the program branched around it. Only the second is control
-- flow, and until this layer existed nothing could tell them apart.
--
-- IT WRITES INTO [code], like the call graph and the modules layer, and not
-- into a ledger of its own. The dataflow layer has `[flow]` because it answers
-- a different KIND of question — what a value may be, rather than what the
-- syntax is — and everything here is a fact about a position in the tree.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 1. A POSITION THAT ONLY RUNS SOMETIMES. One row per (parent, arm), and the
--    vocabulary is closed: `guard_kind` below is what this layer claims to know
--    about, and `guard_unmodelled[audit]` reports a kind that branches and is
--    not in it.
edb(guard_kind).            -- guard_kind(Kind, Field)   an arm that may be skipped
guard_kind(if_statement,               consequent).
guard_kind(if_statement,               alternate).
guard_kind(conditional_expression,     consequent).
guard_kind(conditional_expression,     alternate).
-- `a && b` and `a || b` and `a ?? b` evaluate the RIGHT operand only sometimes;
-- the left always runs. This is the same operator-carries-the-meaning lesson
-- the era layer paid for, arriving where it does not matter: all three
-- short-circuit, so the KIND is enough here and no `operator` guard is needed.
guard_kind(logical_expression,         right).
-- A LOOP BODY MAY RUN ZERO TIMES — except one. `do { } while (c)` runs its body
-- BEFORE testing, so it guards nothing at all, and putting it here would make
-- `may_not_run` report functions that always run. The distinction is in the
-- spec and not in the shape: all five loops look identical in the tree.
guard_kind(for_of_statement,           body).
guard_kind(for_statement,              body).
-- ...AND ITS UPDATE, 2026-09-08 (w_update_and_literals). A C-style `for` has
-- FOUR children and only one of them was on this table. `init` always runs and
-- `test` runs at least once, so neither belongs here — but `update` runs ZERO
-- times when the test is false to begin with, and zero times more when the
-- body leaves by a `break`, a `return` or a throw. It is skippable in exactly
-- the sense `body` is.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE THE ROW, on the one C-style `for` this corpus already had
-- (alpha.mjs:845, `for (let j = 0; j < 2; j += 1)`): `guard_arm` named the
-- body and nothing else, and `guarded[code]` was EMPTY for the `j += 1`. The
-- model positively asserted that a for-update runs — the same direction as
-- `pastLabelledBlock` one item ago, and the direction this layer documents as
-- the dangerous one, because a CALL written there (`for (…; …; m = step(m))`)
-- was `reached_unguarded` and therefore kept out of `may_not_run`.
--
-- THE ITEM THAT FOUND IT IS `update_expression`'s, and that is not a
-- coincidence: `i++` is what a for-update is almost always written as, and
-- nobody had looked at the slot while the form that inhabits it was not in the
-- vocabulary. The row is on `for_statement` because that is whose field it is,
-- and it answers for every expression written there.
guard_kind(for_statement,              update).
guard_kind(for_in_statement,           body).
guard_kind(while_statement,            body).
-- A DEFAULT VALUE RUNS ONLY IF THE ARGUMENT IS ABSENT. `function f(n, made =
-- fallbackMaker(n))` calls `fallbackMaker` at exactly the call sites that omit
-- the second argument, so the `right` of an `assignment_pattern` is an arm
-- skipped by a condition in the same sense a `?:` alternate is — and this is
-- the whole reason `assignment_pattern` is more than a dataflow join.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE THE ROW: with the fixture in and this line absent,
-- `fallbackMaker` was NOT in `may_not_run` although its only call site is
-- inside a default. The `left` is deliberately not an arm: a parameter's NAME
-- is not conditional, only its value is.
guard_kind(assignment_pattern,         right).
-- A CASE BODY is skipped unless the switch selects it, and `consequent` is an
-- ARRAY here — which is why `guard_arm` below reads any index rather than 0.
guard_kind(switch_case,                consequent).
-- THE EXCEPTION MACHINERY, and only one of its three parts is a guard. The
-- `block` always runs; the `finalizer` always runs; the HANDLER runs only if
-- something inside the block threw. Filing the block as guarded would have said
-- that the ordinary path is conditional, which is the wrong way round.
guard_kind(try_statement,              handler).

-- ANY INDEX, not zero. A singular field is index 0 and reads the same; an array
-- field — `switch_case`'s `consequent` — has one arm per statement, and reading
-- only index 0 would have guarded the first statement of a case and no other.
guard_arm[code](P, A) :- guard_kind(K, Field), ast_node[code](P, K, _, _),
                         ast_child[code](P, Field, _, A).

-- A FIELD INITIALISER IS AN ARM SKIPPED BY THE ABSENCE OF A `new`, 2026-09-08 —
-- queue item w_class_fields, and the row is NOT in `guard_kind` because the
-- condition is an ATTRIBUTE and not a field.
--
-- MEASURED BY RUNNING THE SHAPE, which is what split one kind into two answers:
--
--   class Never { unused = mark(); static tag = mark(); }   // defined, never `new`ed
--   -> ["never:staticfield"]
--
-- A STATIC field initialiser runs when the class DEFINITION is evaluated, so it
-- is exactly as conditional as the statement the class sits in and guarding it
-- would report a call that always runs as one that may not. A NON-STATIC one
-- runs once per construction and zero times if the class is never constructed,
-- which is a may-not-run in the layer's own sense.
--
-- DERIVED FROM `transfer_mechanism` rather than from a second hand-written list
-- of the two kinds, on the pattern `suspend_at` and `label_target` already use:
-- the next kind filed under `per_construction` is covered without anybody
-- remembering, and `guard_unmodelled[audit]` reddens if the mechanism is claimed
-- with no rule reaching the kinds that carry it.
field_init[code](P, V) :- transfer_mechanism(K, per_construction),
                          ast_node[code](P, K, _, _), ast_attr[code](P, static, false),
                          ast_child[code](P, value, 0, V).
guard_arm[code](P, V)  :- field_init[code](P, V).

-- ...and everything inside such an arm inherits the condition.
guarded[code](A) :- guard_arm[code](_, A).
guarded[code](N) :- guard_arm[code](_, A), ast_within[code](A, N).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 2. WHAT IT SAYS ABOUT CALLS. A site inside a guarded position may not run,
--    and a function every one of whose call sites is guarded may never be
--    entered at all.
--
-- THE NEGATION IS LAST, and that is not style: written before the literal that
-- binds `F`, it would be evaluated with `F` unbound and read as "nothing
-- unguarded resolves anywhere". Measured yesterday on `member_value`, recorded
-- as f_body_order_changes_the_answer_and_whynot_cannot_see_it, and the reason
-- the order is worth a comment on every rule of this shape until the kernel is
-- fixed.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- STATEMENT ORDER, 2026-09-05 — queue item w_cf_abrupt_transfer, and the last
-- mechanism in the table that had no rule.
--
-- Every other rule in this layer reads a parent/child POSITION: an arm of an
-- `if`, the body of a loop. This one needs the ORDER of siblings, and the order
-- has been on the store since the scanner was written — `ast_child(B, body, I,
-- S)` carries the index. Nothing had ever read it.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE WRITING, and it corrected the queue: the corpus contained
-- ZERO statements sitting syntactically after a return, throw, break or
-- continue in the same block, so this item had nothing to be tested against and
-- a fixture had to come first. See also the witness correction below.
edb(abrupt_kind).
abrupt_kind(return_statement).
abrupt_kind(throw_statement).
abrupt_kind(break_statement).
abrupt_kind(continue_statement).

-- WHERE THIS RULE COULD NOT LOOK, asked of the rule before it was believed and
-- answered by the scanner rather than by reading it: a SWITCH CASE holds its
-- statements under `consequent`, not `body`. `case 1: g(); break; h();` left
-- `h` unguarded, and no audit here said a word — the rule simply matched
-- nothing. The field is vocabulary now instead of a constant inside one rule,
-- which is the move this repository already made for guard arms.
edb(stmt_seq_field).
stmt_seq_field(body).
stmt_seq_field(consequent).

-- THE ARM THAT USED TO STAND HERE IS THE BASE CASE OF A CLOSURE NOW, 2026-09-09
-- (w_cf_completion). It read
--
--     abrupt_at[code](B, F, I) :- abrupt_kind(K), ast_node[code](S, K, _, _),
--                                 stmt_seq_field(F), ast_child[code](B, F, I, S).
--
-- — the statement in the list must BE the transfer — and the item this file
-- opened for it says where that cannot look: a `return` inside a nested block,
-- in both arms of an `if`, or under a label completes the ENCLOSING region and
-- this arm sees none of the three. `completes_abruptly` in COMPLETION below is
-- that arm's first literal made recursive; the arm itself moved there, so that
-- there is exactly one rule concluding this shape rather than two of which one
-- subsumes the other.

-- ORDERED FOR COST: `abrupt_at` is a handful of rows and binds B and F, so the
-- second literal is a probe rather than a walk over every containment pair.
-- THE FIELD IS CARRIED, not re-quantified: an abrupt in a case's `consequent`
-- must not reach a statement in the same node's `body`.
after_abrupt[code](S) :- abrupt_at[code](B, F, I), ast_child[code](B, F, J, S), I < J.

-- A SUSPENSION IS A POINT AFTER WHICH THE REST MAY NOT RUN, and until
-- 2026-09-07 this layer WAIVED the question with the reason
-- `a_control_returns_so_the_site_still_runs`. That reason is a claim about the
-- PROGRAM, not about the language: control comes back only if the awaited thing
-- settles and only if a generator is asked for another value. Every await in
-- the corpus settled, so the waiver had never been exercised — and a fixture
-- that suspends forever put a function the model says runs, and the runtime
-- never entered, in front of the acceptance gate within one run.
--
-- DERIVED FROM `transfer_mechanism`, NOT COPIED. The vocabulary already files
-- `await_expression` and `yield_expression` under `suspend`, and a second hand
-- written list is the defect `orphan_claim` paid for: the next kind filed under
-- `suspend` is covered here without anybody remembering.
--
-- IT IS NOT AN `abrupt_at` ARM, and the distinction is the whole point of
-- having two relations. After a `return` the rest NEVER runs; after an `await`
-- the rest runs unless the promise never settles. Both land in `guarded`, which
-- this layer documents as a MAY-not-run, but writing a suspension as abrupt
-- would put a NEVER in a relation whose own comment says it is weakening a
-- never into a may.
--
-- CONFINED TO THE SUSPENDING FUNCTION, exactly as the throwing-call arm below
-- is: without `nearest_v` an `await` inside a function would guard every
-- statement after that function's own declaration at module level, which is a
-- suspension of the module and is not what happened.
suspend_at[code](B, F, I) :- transfer_mechanism(K, suspend), ast_node[code](X, K, _, _),
                             nearest_v[flow](G, X), ast_within[code](G, S),
                             ast_within[code](S, X),
                             stmt_seq_field(F), ast_child[code](B, F, I, S).
after_suspend[code](S) :- suspend_at[code](B, F, I), ast_child[code](B, F, J, S), I < J.

-- A SEQUENCE FIELD THE SCANNER NEVER EMITS — the same typo hole `guard_arm_unseen`
-- closes for arms, and the one this rule was carrying invisibly until the switch
-- case was measured.
stmt_seq_unseen[audit](F) :- stmt_seq_field(F), not ast_child[code](_, F, 0, _).

-- THE MUTANT SET, run before the item was closed. Five directed at the rule:
-- dropping `I < J`, reversing it, losing one `abrupt_kind`, dropping the
-- containment closure — all four killed. The fifth SURVIVED and its survival is
-- structural rather than a corpus gap: replacing `F` with `_` on both sides,
-- so an abrupt in one field could reach a statement in another, changes nothing
-- HERE and cannot change anything in JavaScript, because no node kind carries
-- two multi-child fields at once — `switch_case` has only `consequent`, every
-- block-like kind only `body`. Carrying F is defence against a future scanner,
-- not against the language, and saying so is cheaper than a mutant that will
-- never go red.
--
-- ...and then the question that actually paid: WHERE IS THIS RULE STRUCTURALLY
-- UNABLE TO LOOK. Four shapes were built and all four came back blind, because
-- this rule reads SIBLINGS and each of them completes an enclosing region
-- instead: a `return` in a nested block, a `return` in both arms of an `if`, a
-- `return` under a label, and a call to a function that never returns. The
-- first three are w_cf_completion; the fourth is w_exn_propagation, and it is
-- the one that corrected this item's own witness.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A LABEL IS A BOUNDARY AN ORDINARY BREAK CANNOT CROSS, 2026-09-08 — queue item
-- w_labelled_control.
--
-- MEASURED BEFORE A LINE OF THIS WAS WRITTEN, on a fixture built for the
-- purpose because the corpus held ZERO labels: with `break outer` in the inner
-- of two nested loops, the callees `after_abrupt[code]` named were
--
--   after neverCased neverReached pastBlockBreak pastBreak pastContinue
--   pastDebugger pastEmpty pastInnerBreak pastPlainBreak unlit unreadable
--
-- ...and that list is not a memory of a world that has gone: deleting the arm
-- below reproduces it exactly, which is mutant l1 in
-- test/js-controlflow.test.ts. Written down once from the pre-rule world, it
-- would have been a witness with no world the moment the fixture grew — and it
-- did grow twice while this item was open.
--
-- `pastBreak` and `pastContinue` are there and `pastLabelledBreak`,
-- `pastLabelledContinue` and `pastLabelledBlock` are not. So THE MODEL WAS
-- TREATING A LABELLED BREAK AS AN ORDINARY ONE: it killed the siblings in the
-- break's own statement list, which a plain `break` does too, and stopped. The
-- whole content of a label — leaving a statement further out — was invisible.
--
-- AND ONE OF THE THREE WAS NOT MERELY MISSING FROM `after_abrupt`. A labelled
-- LOOP body is a `guard_kind` arm, so `pastLabelledBreak` was in `guarded`
-- anyway and the may-set was already hedging. A labelled BLOCK is not a loop,
-- not an arm and not a switch: `pastLabelledBlock` was in NEITHER `guarded` nor
-- `may_not_run`, so the model positively asserted that a statement runs which
-- the only path through its own block skips. A may-set that is too narrow is a
-- claim rather than a hedge, and this was the narrow direction.
--
-- THE LABEL IS READ, NOT THE KEYWORD. `label_ref` is the join that makes the
-- negative control possible: an unlabelled `break` has no `label` child, so it
-- derives nothing here and `beyondPlainBreak` stays out. A rule written over
-- `break_statement` would have stopped telling the two forms apart.
label_name[code](LS, N)   :- ast_node[code](LS, labeled_statement, _, _),
                             ast_child[code](LS, label, 0, I), ast_attr[code](I, name, N).
-- DERIVED FROM `abrupt_kind` rather than from a second hand-written list of the
-- two kinds that carry a label, which is the defect `orphan_claim` paid for.
-- It costs nothing in precision: measured over the fixtures, `break` and
-- `continue` are the only kinds in the language with a `label` CHILD at all —
-- `return` and `throw` carry `argument`, and `labeled_statement` carries its own
-- `label` and is not an abrupt kind.
label_ref[code](X, N)     :- abrupt_kind(K), ast_node[code](X, K, _, _),
                             ast_child[code](X, label, 0, I), ast_attr[code](I, name, N).
-- THE TARGET IS UNIQUE AND THE GRAMMAR IS WHY, not a tie-break of ours. Two
-- labels of the same name nested one inside the other are a SyntaxError
-- ("Label 'outer' has already been declared"), so at most one `labeled_statement`
-- with a given name can contain a given reference; and `outer: f(); outer: g();`
-- is legal precisely because neither contains the other.
label_target[code](X, LS) :- label_ref[code](X, N), label_name[code](LS, N),
                             ast_within[code](LS, X).

-- ...AND THE WALK STOPS AT THE LABEL, which is the precision the item is about.
-- `S` ranges over the statements BETWEEN the reference and its target, so the
-- statements after the labelled statement itself — `beyondLabel`, `afterBlock` —
-- are never reached: their parent list is outside `LS` and `ast_within(LS, S)`
-- excludes it. NO FUNCTION-BOUNDARY LITERAL IS NEEDED here, unlike the
-- suspension and throwing-call arms, and again the grammar is the reason: a
-- label is not in scope inside a nested function, so `outer: for (...) { const
-- f = () => { break outer; }; }` does not parse and no store can hold it.
--
-- ORDERED FOR COST, on the pattern this file already uses for `throwing_call`
-- and `accessor_of`: `label_target` is a handful of rows and binds BOTH X and
-- LS, so the two containment literals are probes from a named node rather than
-- a scan over every containment pair in the corpus.
abrupt_at[code](B, F, I)  :- label_target[code](X, LS), ast_within[code](LS, S),
                             ast_within[code](S, X), stmt_seq_field(F),
                             ast_child[code](B, F, I, S).

-- WHERE THIS ARM IS STRUCTURALLY UNABLE TO LOOK, asked of it before it was
-- believed and answered with a shape rather than a hope: `if (c) break outer;`
-- puts a GUARD between the reference and the target, and everything after the
-- `if` in the enclosing lists is then reported by a relation whose own comment
-- says NEVER when the honest word is MAY. That is not new with this arm — the
-- `throwing_call` arm below has walked up through guard arms since 2026-09-06 —
-- and it is recorded rather than repeated silently, as
-- f_after_abrupt_says_never_and_the_walking_arms_say_may. Both
-- consumers of `after_abrupt` reach it through `guarded`, which is a MAY-set, so
-- nothing downstream reads the stronger word. The fixture for this item breaks
-- unconditionally so that its own witness does not depend on the looser reading.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- COMPLETION, 2026-09-09 — queue item w_cf_completion, and the item's own note
-- is the design: this is NOT a ninth mechanism. The mechanism table already
-- files `return`, `throw`, `break` and `continue` under `abrupt`; what was
-- missing is the CLOSURE — the recursive question *does this statement complete
-- normally?* — which every arm above answers only for the four kinds that ARE
-- the transfer.
--
-- THE THREE SHAPES WERE MEASURED BLIND BEFORE A LINE OF THIS WAS WRITTEN, on a
-- probe with one function per shape and a distinctly named callee after each:
-- `after_abrupt` 1 (the labelled escape, which the arm above reaches) and the
-- callee list EMPTY — `pastNestedReturn`, `pastBothArms` and `pastLabelledReturn`
-- were all outside `may_not_run` and outside `guarded`. That is the item's
-- statement reproduced rather than quoted, and mutants c1, c2 and c3 in
-- test/js-controlflow.test.ts are that world reconstructed one arm at a time.
--
-- IT IS A SOUND UNDER-APPROXIMATION, on the same doctrine as `always_throws`
-- in A CALL IS AN EXIT below: a statement this relation does NOT name may
-- still complete abruptly, and the cost of that is a `guarded` set too small —
-- which is the
-- narrow direction the layer already documents. What it must never do is name a
-- statement that CAN complete normally, and each arm below is the language's own
-- rule for its kind rather than a heuristic.
edb(completion_kind).       -- completion_kind(K)  a kind whose abrupt completion this closure DERIVES
completion_kind(block_statement).
completion_kind(if_statement).
completion_kind(labeled_statement).

-- (1) THE BASE, and it is exactly the four kinds `abrupt_at` used to read
--     directly. A `return` completes abruptly by being a `return`.
completes_abruptly[code](S) :- abrupt_kind(K), ast_node[code](S, K, _, _).

-- (2) A BLOCK COMPLETES ABRUPTLY IF ANY STATEMENT IN IT DOES. Not the LAST one:
--     a statement that completes abruptly is the last one that runs, whatever
--     follows it in the list, so quantifying over the whole body is the language
--     and not a shortcut.
--
--     ORDERED FOR COST, on the pattern this file uses everywhere: seed from
--     `completes_abruptly` (a handful of rows), walk UP one edge to the parent,
--     then check the parent's kind. Written the other way round the first
--     literal is every block statement in the corpus.
completes_abruptly[code](B) :- completes_abruptly[code](S), ast_child[code](B, body, _, S),
                               ast_node[code](B, block_statement, _, _).

-- (3) AN `if` COMPLETES ABRUPTLY ONLY IF BOTH ARMS EXIST AND BOTH DO, which is
--     the whole content of the second shape. `if (c) return;` with no alternate
--     completes normally when `c` is false, and this rule says so by REQUIRING
--     the `alternate` child — an `if` without one derives nothing here.
completes_abruptly[code](S) :- completes_abruptly[code](C), ast_child[code](S, consequent, 0, C),
                               ast_node[code](S, if_statement, _, _),
                               ast_child[code](S, alternate, 0, A), completes_abruptly[code](A).

-- (4) A LABELLED STATEMENT COMPLETES ABRUPTLY IF ITS BODY DOES **AND NOTHING
--     BREAKS OUT OF IT**, and the negative half is the whole reason a label
--     needs an arm of its own rather than falling out of (2). `lbl: { return n; }`
--     never completes normally; `esc: { if (c) break esc; return n; }` completes
--     normally on exactly the path that takes the break, and a closure that
--     ignored the escape would say the statement after `esc:` is unreachable —
--     a claim, in the narrow direction, about a statement that runs.
--
--     `label_target` IS REUSED RATHER THAN RE-DERIVED. It already joins a
--     reference to the labelled statement BY NAME, and the grammar makes that
--     target unique (a nested duplicate label is a SyntaxError). A second walk
--     here would be the `orphan_claim` shape this file has paid for twice.
label_escaped[code](LS)      :- label_target[code](_, LS).
completes_abruptly[code](LS) :- completes_abruptly[code](S), ast_child[code](LS, body, 0, S),
                                ast_node[code](LS, labeled_statement, _, _),
                                not label_escaped[code](LS).

-- ...AND THE CONSUMER IS THE ARM THAT USED TO READ `abrupt_kind` DIRECTLY.
-- Replacing it rather than adding beside it is deliberate: the base case above
-- IS the old arm, so the rows it produced are all still produced, and a second
-- copy would be a rule no mutant could kill because the other one covers it.
abrupt_at[code](B, F, I) :- completes_abruptly[code](S), stmt_seq_field(F),
                            ast_child[code](B, F, I, S).

-- A KIND THIS CLOSURE CLAIMS AND NEVER DECIDES. Same shape as
-- `guard_arm_unseen`, `stmt_seq_unseen` and `export_kind_unseen`, and it covers
-- two failures with one row: an arm misspelled or deleted (the rule stops
-- firing) and a corpus with no site for it (the arm was never exercised). Both
-- read as "the closure is smaller than it says it is", which is the narrow
-- direction, and neither is visible from any count of `after_abrupt`.
completion_decided[code](K)    :- completes_abruptly[code](S), ast_node[code](S, K, _, _).
completion_unreached[audit](K) :- completion_kind(K), ast_node[code](_, K, _, _),
                                  not completion_decided[code](K).

-- WHERE THIS CLOSURE IS STRUCTURALLY UNABLE TO LOOK, asked of it before it was
-- believed and answered with a TABLE rather than a sentence. SEVEN statement
-- kinds over THREE questions can hold a `return` and still complete normally,
-- and each of them is DECLARED here rather than left to fall through (2)'s
-- block arm silently. Four of the seven have a site of their own in
-- test/fixtures/js-call/shapes.ts.txt — a `while`, a `do`/`while`, a `switch`
-- and a `try`, each holding a block that completes abruptly — and the three
-- remaining loop kinds are the same question with the same answer:
edb(completion_deferred).   -- completion_deferred(K, Reason)
-- A loop body may run zero times, and whether it runs at all is the loop's
-- CONDITION — which is the same thing `guard_kind` already refuses to decide for
-- every loop kind in the table.
completion_deferred(while_statement,    a_condition_this_layer_cannot_decide).
completion_deferred(for_statement,      a_condition_this_layer_cannot_decide).
completion_deferred(for_in_statement,   a_condition_this_layer_cannot_decide).
completion_deferred(for_of_statement,   a_condition_this_layer_cannot_decide).
-- ...and `do_while_statement` runs its body once, so a `return` in it really
-- does complete the loop abruptly — EXCEPT that a `continue` in the same body
-- does not, and telling the two apart needs the completion REASON (return /
-- break / continue) that this relation deliberately does not carry. Declared
-- open rather than modelled from the half of the question that is easy.
completion_deferred(do_while_statement, a_completion_reason_this_relation_does_not_carry).
-- A `switch` completes abruptly only if every case does AND there is a
-- `default`, and a `break` inside a case completes the SWITCH normally — the
-- same reason/kind distinction as the loop above, one level harder.
completion_deferred(switch_statement,   a_completion_reason_this_relation_does_not_carry).
-- A `try` whose block completes abruptly may still complete normally, because
-- the handler can swallow the transfer; `finally` can also OVERRIDE it. Neither
-- is decidable from the shape of the statement.
completion_deferred(try_statement,      a_handler_that_may_swallow_the_transfer).

completion_known(K) :- completion_kind(K).
completion_known(K) :- completion_deferred(K, _).
completion_known(K) :- abrupt_kind(K).

-- ...AND THE GATE THAT KEEPS THAT TABLE HONEST: a statement kind that holds an
-- abrupt completion INSIDE ITS OWN CONTROL REGION, sits in a statement list
-- itself, and is in neither list. It reads as "nobody looked", and the answer it
-- is silently given is *completes normally* — the narrow direction again.
--
-- THE FUNCTION BOUNDARY IS THE PRECISION CLAUSE, and without it this gate is
-- noise: `const f = () => { return 1; };` is a `variable_declaration` in a
-- statement list containing a `return`, and every module in the corpus is full
-- of them. Mutant c11 measures exactly that — five kinds, all of them a function
-- in disguise.
--
-- THE CANDIDATE COMES FIRST AND THE BOUNDARY IS ASKED OF IT, which is an
-- ORDERING decision and was measured rather than reasoned. Written the other way
-- round — `completes_abruptly(S), fn_node_v(G), ast_within(G, S), ast_within(P, G)`
-- — the second literal is unbound, so the pair relation is built over EVERY
-- function in the corpus crossed with every abrupt completion and then filtered:
-- one world went from 8.8 s to 21.7 s, and 13 of those seconds were this one
-- rule. Seeding from `completion_outer`, which is only the kinds the table does
-- not know, is the same answer for a twentieth of the work.
completion_outer[code](P, S)      :- completes_abruptly[code](S), ast_within[code](P, S),
                                     ast_node[code](P, K, _, _), not completion_known(K),
                                     not fn_node_v[flow](P),
                                     stmt_seq_field(F), ast_child[code](_, F, _, P).
completion_fn_between[code](P, S) :- completion_outer[code](P, S), ast_within[code](P, G),
                                     fn_node_v[flow](G), ast_within[code](G, S).
completion_unaccounted[audit](K)  :- completion_outer[code](P, S), ast_node[code](P, K, _, _),
                                     not completion_fn_between[code](P, S).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A CALL IS AN EXIT, 2026-09-06 — queue item w_exception_flow, and it corrects
-- an attribution this file made TWO ITERATIONS AGO. When w_cf_abrupt_transfer
-- closed, `useTry -> after` was moved from that item to w_exn_propagation (28,
-- the TRANSITIVE closure) on the reasoning that `after` follows a call that
-- throws. Measured now: `thrower`'s own body contains the throw, with no
-- handler and no return, so nothing has to propagate anywhere. It is item 25's
-- half — a call that leaves by THROWING rather than returning — and the
-- transitive item was one step too far. Ninth measurement in this session to
-- correct an attribution, and the first to correct one of my own corrections.
--
-- A SOUND UNDER-APPROXIMATION, and the soundness is the whole design. "F always
-- throws" is completion analysis in general (w_cf_completion), but one case is
-- decidable from syntax alone: NO `return` anywhere in the body, and a `throw`
-- at the TOP LEVEL of it. Then control cannot leave the body by returning
-- (there is no return), cannot fall off the end (the top-level throw is reached
-- or an inner throw fires first), and cannot skip past the throw. The error
-- direction is the safe one: a function that does always throw but has a return
-- somewhere is simply not named, so a call to it stays live.
-- ORDERED FOR COST, MEASURED 2026-09-09 AND APPLIED BY MEASUREMENT.
-- Leading with `fn_node(F)` left R unbound, so this body enumerated EVERY
-- return statement in the corpus against every function and then filtered by
-- containment. It was 94 per cent of the whole control-flow layer's read cost:
-- 1 703 150 rows on one path, where the next heaviest clause in the layer is
-- worth 780. Named by ABLATION over all 28 clauses in this file that mention
-- `ast_node`, by the per-layer cost gate on its first run.
-- TWO INDEPENDENT CORPORA AGREE. Here: control-flow world 10 088 161 ->
-- 7 742 219 rows, -23.3%, with firings 177 218 and facts 434 149 IDENTICAL and
-- ten named relations equal row for row. A second session the same night
-- measured the same reorder over sixteen files of eslint/lib through a
-- different scanner: total width 5 331 063 -> 3 615 655, fact count identical,
-- and the CURVE it took says time as facts^1.57 becomes facts^1.36.
-- AND IT IS APPLIED ONLY BECAUSE IT WAS RUN. The same reasoning failed twice
-- the same night — `closer_v` 65 per cent WORSE and `tdz_deferred` worth
-- 0.013 per cent — so the argument for this shape is not evidence for it.
-- AND THE OTHER SESSION'S NUMBER, kept because it prices a DIFFERENT quantity
-- and the two together are worth more than either. Mine counts rows handed
-- out by the store; theirs counts accumulator elements reaching the third
-- position, measured from inside the join by scanners/js_cost.ts on sixteen
-- files of eslint/lib: 1 718 888 elements to produce 653 conclusions, 32.4
-- per cent of the whole fixpoint's width. Two instruments, two corpora, one
-- clause, and the repaired body below is BYTE-IDENTICAL in both trees —
-- derived twice independently, which is the only reason that is reassuring
-- rather than merely duplicated.
has_return[code](F)    :- ast_node[code](R, return_statement, _, _),
                          ast_within[code](F, R), fn_node[code](F).
top_throw[code](F)     :- fn_node[code](F), ast_child[code](F, body, 0, B),
                          ast_child[code](B, body, _, S),
                          ast_node[code](S, throw_statement, _, _).
always_throws[code](F) :- top_throw[code](F), not has_return[code](F).

throwing_call[code](C) :- call_site[code](C, _), resolves[code](C, F),
                          always_throws[code](F).
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- AN ACCESSOR IS A CALL WEARING A READ'S SYNTAX, 2026-09-06 — queue item
-- w_cf_accessor, the LAST open cell at this layer.
--
-- THE ITEM'S NOTE SAID THE VALUE LAYER DOES NOT RECORD WHICH PROPERTIES ARE
-- ACCESSORS. Measured: the scanner emits `ast_attr(M, kind, "get"|"set")` for
-- every method, the value layer already reads that exact attribute for
-- `"constructor"` in `own_ctor`, and nothing had ever asked it for `"get"`.
-- Fourth design note in four items to lose to one probe, and the third time
-- this session the answer was a fact that had been on the store all along.
--
-- WHY IT BELONGS TO THIS LAYER RATHER THAN THE VALUE ONE: `o.p` on an accessor
-- TRANSFERS CONTROL, and the value question — what the getter returns — is a
-- different and harder one this does not answer. The value layer currently
-- reads `o.p` as the getter METHOD itself, which is wrong about the value and
-- silent about the transfer; naming the transfer is what this cell asks for.
edb(accessor_kind).
accessor_kind("get").
accessor_kind("set").

accessor_of[flow](Obj, Key, M) :- member_value[flow](Obj, Key, M),
                                  accessor_kind(K), ast_attr[code](M, kind, K).
-- ORDERED FOR COST: `accessor_of` is a handful of rows and binds Obj and Key,
-- so the receiver walk is a probe rather than a scan of every member read.
-- ...AND `selects` BEFORE `member_node_v`, which the note above got half
-- right. Putting `accessor_of` first does bind Obj and Key, but the NEXT
-- premise was `member_node_v(N)` with N sharing nothing with them — every
-- accessor laid beside every member read, and `selects` filtering afterwards.
-- Measured by scanners/js_cost.ts on sixteen files of eslint/lib: 88 980
-- accumulator elements to conclude NOTHING. Asking `selects(N, Key)` with Key
-- already bound names the reads of that key and leaves `member_node_v(N)` a
-- check, which is what "a probe rather than a scan" was meant to be.
accessor_read[code](N, M) :- accessor_of[flow](Obj, Key, M),
                             selects[flow](N, Key), member_node_v[flow](N),
                             ast_child[code](N, object, 0, O),
                             may_be_node[flow](O, Obj).
-- ...AND A PRIVATE ACCESSOR, 2026-09-08 (w_class_fields). `get #face()` is a
-- `class_private_method` carrying `kind: "get"`, and `this.#face` is a member
-- read that runs it — the same transfer with none of the same machinery, because
-- a private name is bound LEXICALLY and never reaches `member_value` or
-- `selects`. NO RECEIVER LITERAL IS NEEDED and that is not a shortcut: the class
-- body that declares `#face` is the only place `this.#face` can be written at
-- all, so `private_binds` has already decided both halves that
-- `accessor_of` + `may_be_node` decide for a public one.
accessor_read[code](N, M) :- private_binds[code](N, M),
                             accessor_kind(K), ast_attr[code](M, kind, K).

-- ...AND IT COMPOSES WITH THE ITEM BEFORE IT. A getter that always throws makes
-- the READ an exit, with no call syntax anywhere at the site. This arm is the
-- reason the accessor question is worth answering at this layer at all: without
-- it the transfer would be a fact nothing consumes, which is the shape this
-- repository keeps finding on its own store.
throwing_call[code](N) :- accessor_read[code](N, M), always_throws[code](M).

-- ...AND THE CALL GRAPH HAS TO SAY SO, which the first version did not and the
-- EXECUTION ORACLE caught within one run: UNSOUND went 0 -> 2, the first
-- unsoundness of the session, naming `useGauge -> get broken` and
-- `useGauge -> get reading`. The runtime enters both getters and the model
-- derived no edge to either, because `gauge.broken` is not a `call_site` under
-- any spelling. A transfer the control-flow layer names and the call graph
-- denies is half a model, and the half that is wrong is the one every consumer
-- reads. The member_expression cells at the call-graph layer are already
-- `handled`; this makes that claim true of one more site rather than changing a
-- verdict.
resolves[code](N, M) :- accessor_read[code](N, M).
-- ...and it has to be a SITE, or the edge has no caller. `nearest_fn` ranges
-- over `site[code]`, and `site` is `call_site` plus `transfer_site` — the latter
-- keyed by KIND, so declaring `member_expression` a transfer kind would make
-- every property read in the corpus a transfer site. The arm is on `site`
-- directly and carries only the reads that really do run a function: two, here.
site[code](N) :- accessor_read[code](N, _).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- A DESTRUCTURING FORM HIDES A CALL, 2026-09-08 — queue item
-- w_destructuring_hides_a_call, and it is `accessor_call` wearing two other
-- syntaxes plus the iterator protocol wearing three.
--
-- WHAT UNBLOCKED IT WAS NOT WHAT THE ITEM WAITED ON. It waited on
-- `w_env_api_surface` on the ground that naming the receiver's iterator is a
-- standard-library question. MEASURED against the surface that item generated,
-- with a positive control: `lib_member(P, K, R)` carries 130 rows over eight
-- prototypes, and `lib_member(P, "iterator", R)`, `lib_member(P,
-- "Symbol.iterator", R)` and `lib_member(P, "next", R)` are each ZERO. The
-- surface is keyed by NAME and a well-known symbol is not a name, so the
-- blocker was never lifted — it was the WRONG BLOCKER.
--
-- THE RECEIVER DECIDES, NOT THE SYNTAX, and that is the measurement this item
-- turns on. Ten cases run against V8's own stack (the same instrument
-- test/js-callgraph.ts uses, nothing here reads a rule):
--
--   { notch } = dial            dial has `get notch`     -> get notch RAN
--   { plain, ...rest } = dial   two getters left over    -> BOTH RAN
--   { ...dial }                 an object spread         -> BOTH RAN
--   [first] = spool             spool has [Symbol.iterator] -> iterator, next RAN
--   [...spool] / f(...spool)    the same                 -> iterator, next RAN
--   [...[a, b]] / [x] = [a]     a PLAIN ARRAY            -> NO USER CODE RAN
--   { ...{ a: 1 } }             a PLAIN OBJECT           -> NO USER CODE RAN
--
-- So the four kinds transfer control when and only when the SOURCE carries user
-- code, which is exactly the condition `accessor_of[flow]` and
-- `member_value[flow](Obj, "iterator", M)` already decide. Every existing
-- destructuring site in this corpus reads a plain array or a plain object, which
-- is why the layer could stand for a week saying they transfer nothing and no
-- oracle contradicted it: the fixtures had no receiver that could.
--
-- AND THE SECOND HOP IS ATTRIBUTABLE, measured the same way: with the fixture's
-- `{ next: namedFn }` shape V8 names the ENCLOSING function as the caller of
-- both the iterator and `next`, for an array pattern, an array spread and a
-- spread argument alike — identical to `for-of`, which is the baseline the same
-- probe ran beside them. So the model can be held to these edges by execution.

-- (1) THE GETTER SIDE. Three kinds read an object's KEYS, and a key backed by a
-- getter is a call — the same sentence `accessor_read` was written for, with a
-- binding rather than a member read in front of it.
-- THE SOURCE A PATTERN READS, and it is DERIVED rather than declared `edb`:
-- every other table in this file with that declaration is authored data, and a
-- relation a rule concludes has no business claiming nobody derives it.
--
-- ONLY THE DECLARATOR FORM: a pattern in a
-- PARAMETER position reads whatever the argument was, which is a call-site
-- question this arm deliberately does not answer, so a parameter pattern
-- derives nothing here and is silent rather than wrong.
pattern_source[code](P, Init) :- ast_node[code](D, variable_declarator, _, _),
                                 ast_child[code](D, id, 0, P),
                                 ast_child[code](D, init, 0, Init).

pattern_accessor[code](P, M) :- ast_node[code](P, object_pattern, _, _),
                                pattern_source[code](P, Init), may_be_node[flow](Init, Obj),
                                pattern_takes[code](P, Key), accessor_of[flow](Obj, Key, M).
-- ...and an object REST runs every getter the pattern did NOT take. The
-- exclusion is the whole content of the arm, exactly as it is for the
-- `member_value` arm this one mirrors in rules/js-dataflow.rofl.
pattern_accessor[code](R, M) :- rest_in_pattern[code](D, R, _),
                                ast_child[code](D, id, 0, P),
                                ast_child[code](D, init, 0, Init), may_be_node[flow](Init, Obj),
                                accessor_of[flow](Obj, Key, M), not pattern_takes[code](P, Key).
-- ...and an object SPREAD runs every getter of what it spreads. `{ ...o, k: v }`
-- overrides a key and still READS it first, so there is no exclusion here and
-- the asymmetry with the arm above is the language's rather than an omission.
pattern_accessor[code](S, M) :- ast_node[code](O, object_expression, _, _),
                                ast_child[code](O, properties, _, S),
                                ast_node[code](S, spread_element, _, _),
                                ast_child[code](S, argument, 0, A), may_be_node[flow](A, Obj),
                                accessor_of[flow](Obj, Key, M).

-- (2) THE ITERABLE SIDE, and it is `for_of_iterates` at three more nodes.
-- WHICH SPREADS ITERATE, keyed by the FIELD the node sits in rather than by its
-- kind, because `spread_element` is one kind doing two different things: in
-- `properties` it copies keys and in `elements`/`arguments` it exhausts an
-- iterator. A rule keyed on the kind alone would say `{ ...o }` calls an
-- iterator, which the ten-case probe above measures as false.
edb(spread_iterable_field).
spread_iterable_field(elements).
spread_iterable_field(arguments).
spread_iterated[code](S) :- ast_node[code](S, spread_element, _, _),
                            spread_iterable_field(F), ast_child[code](_, F, _, S).

pattern_iterates[code](P, M) :- ast_node[code](P, array_pattern, _, _),
                                pattern_source[code](P, Init), may_be_node[flow](Init, Obj),
                                member_value[flow](Obj, "iterator", M), fn_node[code](M).
pattern_iterates[code](S, M) :- spread_iterated[code](S),
                                ast_child[code](S, argument, 0, A), may_be_node[flow](A, Obj),
                                member_value[flow](Obj, "iterator", M), fn_node[code](M).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- HOW THE TWO SIDES REACH THE CALL GRAPH, AND THEY REACH IT DIFFERENTLY. The
-- reason is the one `for_of_iterates` already recorded: `ambiguous_call[audit]`
-- reads a site with two answers as an over-approximation that must be visible,
-- which is right for a callee POSITION and wrong for a node that makes SEVERAL
-- calls all of which are true.
--
--   an ITERABLE position has exactly one `[Symbol.iterator]` per receiver, so
--   two answers there really are two candidate receivers -> `resolves`, which
--   also buys the module-scope case through `top_call` for nothing;
--   an OBJECT position runs one getter PER KEY, so a rest over two getters is
--   two true calls at one node -> `calls` directly, with the top-level arm
--   written out because `const { ... } = kit` at module scope is the shape this
--   corpus already has and `nearest_fn` cannot reach it.
site[code](N)          :- pattern_accessor[code](N, _).
calls[code](Caller, M) :- pattern_accessor[code](N, M), nearest_fn[code](Caller, N).
calls[code](R, M)      :- pattern_accessor[code](N, M), top_call[code](N, R).

-- `site` ON BOTH SIDES, and it is not bookkeeping: `nearest_fn` ranges over
-- `site[code]`, so a `resolves` row at a node nothing calls a site resolves a
-- callee and draws NO EDGE. Measured — the first version of this rule omitted
-- the line below, `pattern_iterates` stood at three rows and the execution
-- oracle reported six missing edges.
site[code](N)          :- pattern_iterates[code](N, _).
resolves[code](N, M)   :- pattern_iterates[code](N, M).
-- ...and the iterator object's `next`, which no syntax names — the same join
-- `for_of_iterates` makes, on the same three relations, because it is the same
-- protocol reached through a different door.
calls[code](Caller, Next) :- pattern_iterates[code](X, M), nearest_fn[code](Caller, X),
                             returns[flow](M, E), may_be_node[flow](E, IterObj),
                             member_value[flow](IterObj, "next", V),
                             may_be_node[flow](V, Next), fn_node[code](Next).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- WHAT PROPAGATES, 2026-09-06 — queue item w_exn_propagation, the transitive
-- half of the exception path and the item that took `useTry -> after` back and
-- forth twice before the measurement settled it.
--
-- FIRST ITEM IN FIVE THAT NEEDED NO NEW FIXTURE. Measured before a line was
-- written: seven throw sites, six of them not caught by a try in their own
-- function, and the closure over the call graph adds FOUR names —
-- `nestedThrow -> useNested -> main`, `topThrowWithReturn -> useWithReturn`,
-- `Fuse -> Lit`. The corpus grew the chain by accident while three earlier
-- items were writing their own witnesses, which is the first time this session
-- the queue's own note was simply right.
--
-- A TRY CATCHES WHAT RUNS IN ITS OWN FUNCTION, and the `nearest_v` equality is
-- not decoration: `try { const f = () => g(); } catch {}` has `g()` inside the
-- try's block TEXTUALLY, and it runs when `f` is called, which is later and
-- elsewhere. Without the equality the model would report that call caught.
try_of[code](TS, F)        :- ast_node[code](TS, try_statement, _, _), nearest_v[flow](F, TS).
in_try_block[code](TS, N)  :- ast_node[code](TS, try_statement, _, _),
                              ast_child[code](TS, block, 0, B), ast_within[code](B, N).
caught_here[code](N)       :- in_try_block[code](TS, N), try_of[code](TS, F),
                              nearest_v[flow](F, N).

-- A THROW THAT LEAVES ITS FUNCTION. Note `rethrown`: its throw sits in a
-- HANDLER, and a clause does not catch what its own handler throws — the
-- `block` field is what `in_try_block` reads, which is the same distinction
-- `thrown_in[flow]` had to make one item ago.
throws_outright[code](F) :- ast_node[code](T, throw_statement, _, _),
                            nearest_v[flow](F, T), not caught_here[code](T).

-- ORDERED FOR COST: `may_throw` is a handful of rows and binds G, so `resolves`
-- is probed by callee rather than enumerated.
may_throw[code](F) :- throws_outright[code](F).
may_throw[code](F) :- may_throw[code](G), resolves[code](C, G), nearest_v[flow](F, C),
                      not caught_here[code](C).

-- ...AND THE VALUE TRAVELS WITH IT, which is what closes the frontier item 25
-- had to declare. `catch_from_call[flow]` named a catch parameter whose source
-- crosses a call edge and handed it to THIS item; the arm below is the answer,
-- and the audit beside it can now say something instead of deferring.
thrown_by[flow](F, V) :- ast_node[code](T, throw_statement, _, _), nearest_v[flow](F, T),
                         not caught_here[code](T), ast_child[code](T, argument, 0, V).
thrown_by[flow](F, V) :- thrown_by[flow](G, V), resolves[code](C, G),
                         nearest_v[flow](F, C), not caught_here[code](C).
caught_value[flow](P, V) :- catch_of[flow](T, H), catch_param[flow](H, P),
                            try_block[flow](T, B), ast_within[code](B, C),
                            resolves[code](C, G), thrown_by[flow](G, V).

-- A SECOND ARM WAS WRITTEN AND REMOVED, and the mutant is why. `super(...)`
-- looked like it needed a hop through `ctor_of[flow]`, because a super call
-- names the CLASS and not the body that runs. Measured: `resolves` already
-- returns the `class_method` for a super call, so the arm never fired — the
-- mutant that deleted it changed NOTHING, on any relation. A rule no corpus
-- exercises cannot go red, so shipping it would have been a capability
-- invisible to every gate here, which is the failure this repository names by
-- name. `super x controlflow` is answered by the arm above.

-- ...AND A TRY STOPS IT. The call is an abrupt transfer for the statement list
-- it sits in, and for every list further out UNTIL a `try` catches it: code
-- after the try runs precisely because the handler caught the throw. Without
-- this clause the rule would report everything after a `try` as unreachable,
-- which is the failure mode `may_not_run` was built to avoid — a may-set that
-- is too WIDE stops being a hedge and becomes a false claim.
try_stops[code](C, T) :- throwing_call[code](C), ast_node[code](T, try_statement, _, _),
                         ast_within[code](T, C).
try_stops[code](C, S) :- try_stops[code](C, T), ast_within[code](S, T).

-- ...AND SO DOES THE FUNCTION BOUNDARY, which the first draft omitted and the
-- corpus caught in one run: without `ast_within(G, S)` the walk up from a
-- throwing call reached the CLASS DECLARATION and then the module's own
-- statement list, so everything after `class Lit` in alpha.mjs was reported
-- unreachable — `after_abrupt` 3 -> 37, `reachable` 110 -> 38. An exit leaves
-- the CURRENT function; whether it leaves the caller too is the transitive
-- question, and w_exn_propagation owns it. The boundary between the two items
-- is exactly the boundary this literal draws.
--
-- ORDERED FOR COST: `throwing_call` is a handful of rows and binds C, so
-- `nearest_v` is a probe and `ast_within` is walked UP from a few nodes rather
-- than over every containment pair.
abrupt_at[code](B, F, I) :- throwing_call[code](C), nearest_v[flow](G, C),
                            ast_within[code](G, S), ast_within[code](S, C),
                            stmt_seq_field(F), ast_child[code](B, F, I, S),
                            not try_stops[code](C, S).

-- A STATEMENT AFTER AN ABRUPT TRANSFER NEVER RUNS, which is stronger than what
-- `guarded` says — `guarded` is a MAY-not-run and this is a NEVER. The weaker
-- word is used deliberately: the layer's vocabulary is the may-set, every
-- consumer reads it that way, and a second stronger relation would need its own
-- consumers before it could be checked. The over-coverage is in the safe
-- direction and is what `may_not_run` is documented to be.
guarded[code](S) :- after_abrupt[code](S).
guarded[code](N) :- after_abrupt[code](S), ast_within[code](S, N).
-- ...and the same two arms for a SUSPENSION, which is a may rather than a never
-- and therefore belongs in this relation more exactly than the abrupt does.
guarded[code](S) :- after_suspend[code](S).
guarded[code](N) :- after_suspend[code](S), ast_within[code](S, N).

guarded_call[code](C)      :- call_site[code](C, _), guarded[code](C).
reached_unguarded[code](F) :- resolves[code](C, F), not guarded[code](C).
may_not_run[code](F)       :- resolves[code](_, F), not reached_unguarded[code](F).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- TRANSITIVE REACHABILITY, 2026-09-06 — queue item w_cf_reachability.
--
-- `may_not_run` is LOCAL: it asks whether a function's own call sites are
-- guarded and stops there. Whether the CALLER runs is the transitive question,
-- and the item's note said guessing at entry points would answer something
-- nobody asked, so the walk was declared absent rather than approximated.
--
-- THE MEASUREMENT SAID SOMETHING SHARPER THAN THE NOTE, and it decided the
-- design. Written without an entry point the question is not merely
-- under-determined, it is UNSTRATIFIED and the kernel refuses the program:
--
--     live(F) :- resolves(C, F), not guarded(C), nearest_v(G, C), not dead(G).
--     dead(F) :- resolves(_, F), not live(F).
--     -> "round 5 settled nothing while dead_fn, live_call remained"
--
-- An entry point is not a convenience here. It is what turns a cycle through
-- negation into a positive least fixpoint, and the refusal is the rule that
-- says so — measured before a line of this was written.
--
-- AND IT NEED NOT BE GUESSED. A module's EXPORT SURFACE is its entry surface,
-- and the store already carries it. That is a fact about the language rather
-- than about this corpus, which is the whole difference between a seed and an
-- approximation. `export_all_declaration` is deliberately NOT here: it
-- re-exports someone else's names and introduces no local entry.
edb(export_kind).
export_kind(export_named_declaration).
export_kind(export_default_declaration).

fn_node[code](F) :- fn_name[code](F, _).
in_fn[code](N)   :- nearest_v[flow](_, N).

-- THE SURFACE IS CONTAINMENT, NOT A PATH. `export function f(){}` puts the
-- function under `declaration`; `export const g = () => {}` puts it under
-- declaration/declarations/init; `export default function h(){}` is a third
-- kind entirely. Enumerating the three paths is the shape this repository has
-- twice paid to stop writing. `not in_fn` is what keeps it precise: a closure
-- nested inside an exported function has an enclosing function and is NOT part
-- of the surface, while a method of an exported class has none and IS — anyone
-- who imports the class can call it.
exported_fn[code](F) :- export_kind(K), ast_node[code](E, K, _, _),
                        ast_within[code](E, F), fn_node[code](F), not in_fn[code](F).

-- ...AND THE EXPORT FORM CONTAINMENT CANNOT REACH (w_export_specifier_forms,
-- 2026-09-08). `export { f }` and `export { f as g }` contain no function at
-- all — the declaration's subtree is two identifiers — so the rule above walks
-- it and finds nothing, and until this arm existed a module whose every export
-- went through a specifier list had an EMPTY entry surface.
--
-- MEASURED, on a probe whose three functions were exported only that way:
-- `exported_fn` 0, `entry_point` 0, `reachable` 0, `no_entry_point[audit]`
-- firing on the file, and `may_not_be_reached` naming `helper` — a function
-- that same file exports AND calls. The positive control, the identical three
-- functions written `export function`, gives all three as entry points and an
-- empty `may_not_be_reached`. So this is the dangerous direction the export-kind
-- typo gate already names, arriving through a form rather than a misspelling.
--
-- THE JOIN IS `export_local` FROM rules/js-dataflow.rofl AND NOT A SECOND WALK:
-- that relation already excludes a re-export's `local` (a name in another
-- module) and a type-only export (erased before anything runs), and both of
-- those are wrong answers here too. `not in_fn` is the same precision clause the
-- arm above carries, for the same reason.
exported_fn[code](F) :- export_local[code](L, _, _), may_be_node[flow](L, F),
                        fn_node[code](F), not in_fn[code](F).

entry_point[code](F) :- exported_fn[code](F).

reachable[code](F) :- entry_point[code](F).
-- ...and a call at the top level of a module, which runs on import and has no
-- enclosing function to be reached from.
reachable[code](F) :- resolves[code](C, F), not guarded[code](C), not in_fn[code](C).
reachable[code](F) :- reachable[code](G), nearest_v[flow](G, C), resolves[code](C, F),
                      not guarded[code](C).

-- STILL A MAY-SET, and the name says which one. `may_not_run` asks about a
-- function's own sites; this asks whether anything that reaches it runs at all.
may_not_be_reached[code](F) :- resolves[code](_, F), not reachable[code](F).

-- AN EXPORT KIND THE SCANNER NEVER EMITS — the third instance of the same typo
-- hole, after `guard_arm_unseen` and `stmt_seq_unseen`. A misspelling here
-- silently shrinks the entry surface, and a smaller seed reports live functions
-- as maybe-dead, which is the dangerous direction.
export_kind_unseen[audit](K) :- export_kind(K), not ast_node[code](_, K, _, _).

-- A FILE WITH FUNCTIONS AND NO ENTRY POINT AT ALL. Every function in it comes
-- back `may_not_be_reached`, and that is an artefact of the SEED rather than a
-- finding about the program — a script with no exports is ordinary JavaScript.
-- Named rather than derived silently, because a whole file reported dead looks
-- exactly like a discovery.
-- `has_entry` is [code] and not [audit] on purpose: it is the denominator the
-- gate below ranges over, and a denominator is not a gate. The same distinction
-- `scanned_file` earned in the era layer, and the meta-gate that demands a
-- mutant per audit head is what forced it to be stated here too.
has_entry[code](File)       :- entry_point[code](F), ast_node[code](F, _, File, _).
no_entry_point[audit](File) :- fn_node[code](F), ast_node[code](F, _, File, _),
                               not has_entry[code](File).

-- ONE LEVEL, DECLARED. A function called unguarded from a function that itself
-- may never run is NOT reported: this asks about the site, not about the walk.
-- The transitive form is a reachability question and it wants the layer to have
-- an entry point first — `main` is one here only by convention, and a model
-- that guessed at entry points would be answering a question nobody asked.
-- `may_not_run_transitive` is deliberately absent rather than approximated.

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- 3. THE GATES.

-- A KIND THAT TRANSFERS CONTROL AND IS NOT ACCOUNTED FOR. The first draft had
-- ONE relation, `branching_kind`, and the gate named five kinds in its first
-- run: `await`, `yield`, `return`, and both optional forms. They are not one
-- thing, and putting them in one bucket would have given four different
-- mechanisms one verdict — the defect `s_member_on_other` already paid for.
--
--   SKIP AN ARM        an if/conditional arm, a `&&` right side, a loop body:
--                      the arm may be skipped and everything in it with it.
--                      Modelled, above.
--   SHORT-CIRCUIT      `a?.b()`: nothing is skipped by POSITION — the whole
--                      node stops evaluating when the object is nullish, so
--                      the guarded thing is the site itself, not a child.
--                      Modelled, below.
--   SUSPEND            `await`, `yield`: control leaves and COMES BACK. The
--                      code after them runs; it merely runs later. Waived —
--                      this layer asks whether a site runs, and the answer for
--                      a suspension is yes.
--   ABRUPT             `return` (and `throw`, `break`, `continue` when the
--                      corpus grows them): everything after it in the same
--                      block is unreachable. REAL and NOT MODELLED: it is a
--                      question about statement ORDER within a block, which
--                      nothing in this layer expresses yet.
edb(transfer_mechanism).    -- transfer_mechanism(Kind, Mechanism)
transfer_mechanism(if_statement,               skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(conditional_expression,     skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(logical_expression,         skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(for_of_statement,           skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(optional_call_expression,   short_circuit).
transfer_mechanism(optional_member_expression, short_circuit).
transfer_mechanism(member_expression,          accessor_call).
transfer_mechanism(await_expression,           suspend).
transfer_mechanism(yield_expression,           suspend).
transfer_mechanism(return_statement,           abrupt).
transfer_mechanism(throw_statement,            abrupt).
transfer_mechanism(break_statement,            abrupt).
transfer_mechanism(continue_statement,         abrupt).
transfer_mechanism(while_statement,            skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(for_statement,              skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(for_in_statement,           skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(switch_statement,           skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(switch_case,                skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(try_statement,              skip_arm).
transfer_mechanism(catch_clause,               skip_arm).
-- A FIFTH MECHANISM, and it exists so that `do_while_statement` is not filed
-- under either of the two that would be wrong. Its body runs at least once, so
-- it guards nothing — and it is not a suspension either, because control never
-- leaves. Waived with the reason spelled out rather than left out of the table,
-- because a loop kind missing from `transfer_mechanism` reads as "nobody
-- looked" and this one was looked at.
transfer_mechanism(do_while_statement,         loop_at_least_once).
-- ...and a defaulted parameter, whose `right` is skipped when the argument is
-- supplied. `guard_named` follows from `guard_kind` and needs no row of its own.
transfer_mechanism(assignment_pattern,         skip_arm).
-- A SIXTH MECHANISM, 2026-09-08 (w_labelled_control), and it is a sixth rather
-- than `abrupt` because a `labeled_statement` does not transfer control — it
-- RECEIVES one. Filing it under `abrupt` would say that everything after a
-- label in the same block is unreachable, which is false of every label in the
-- language; filing it under `skip_arm` would say its body may be skipped, which
-- is false too. What it does is BOUND a transfer some other statement performs,
-- and the layer's own doctrine is that four mechanisms in one bucket get one
-- verdict between them.
transfer_mechanism(labeled_statement,          label_boundary).
-- A SEVENTH MECHANISM, 2026-09-08 (w_plugin_gated_kinds), and it replaces a
-- WAIVER rather than filling a gap. `ignored(js, decorator, controlflow,
-- a_no_control_transfer)` stood here until the scanner could see a decorator,
-- and it is false: `@decoOnce class Decorated {}` calls `decoOnce` with the
-- class and a context object at class-definition time. Same shape as
-- `accessor_call` — a construct whose surface is an annotation and whose
-- semantics is a call — and unlike the destructuring cases in
-- `w_destructuring_hides_a_call` it is CLOSABLE, because the callee is named
-- right there in user code and needs no standard-library surface to reach.
--
-- ITS OWN MECHANISM AND NOT `accessor_call`, because when it runs differs: an
-- accessor call happens on every property access and a decorator runs ONCE, at
-- definition, before the decorated thing can be reached at all.
transfer_mechanism(decorator,                  definition_time_call).
-- A SEVENTH MECHANISM, 2026-09-08 (w_class_fields), and it is a seventh for the
-- same reason `label_boundary` was a sixth. A class field's initialiser is not
-- skipped by a CONDITION anywhere in the source — nothing near it branches — and
-- it is not evaluated once either: it runs on every `new` and never if there is
-- none. `skip_arm` would have said a condition decides it, which no reader could
-- then find; `loop_at_least_once` would have said it always runs, which is the
-- narrow direction. Measured by running a class that is defined and never
-- constructed: the STATIC initialiser ran and the instance one did not.
transfer_mechanism(class_property,             per_construction).
transfer_mechanism(class_private_property,     per_construction).
-- AN EIGHTH MECHANISM, 2026-09-08 (w_destructuring_hides_a_call), and it is the
-- first time a KIND in this table carries TWO. `spread_element` in `properties`
-- copies keys and runs getters — `accessor_call`, the mechanism already here —
-- and in `elements` or `arguments` it exhausts an iterator, which is a call into
-- a method the receiver names and neither an accessor nor an arm skipped by a
-- condition. One row per (kind, mechanism) is what lets the table say that
-- rather than making a reader pick which half to write down.
--
-- `for_of_statement` IS HERE FOR THE SAME REASON and its row is a correction:
-- it has carried `skip_arm` alone since the layer opened, which is true of its
-- BODY and silent about `E[Symbol.iterator]()`, a call `for_of_iterates` has
-- modelled at the call-graph layer since 2026-09-07 with no row in this table
-- to say so.
transfer_mechanism(object_pattern,             accessor_call).
transfer_mechanism(rest_element,               accessor_call).
transfer_mechanism(spread_element,             accessor_call).
transfer_mechanism(array_pattern,              iterator_call).
transfer_mechanism(spread_element,             iterator_call).
transfer_mechanism(for_of_statement,           iterator_call).

edb(mechanism_modelled).    -- mechanism_modelled(M)  this layer answers it
mechanism_modelled(skip_arm).
mechanism_modelled(short_circuit).
mechanism_modelled(accessor_call).
mechanism_modelled(abrupt).
-- SUSPEND JOINED 2026-09-07, and it left the waiver rather than the table: the
-- reason `a_control_returns_so_the_site_still_runs` asserted that the promise
-- settles, which this layer cannot know and never checked.
mechanism_modelled(suspend).
-- LABEL_BOUNDARY JOINED 2026-09-08 (w_labelled_control) and it arrived answered
-- rather than open: `label_target` reads the labelled statement by kind and the
-- extra `abrupt_at` arm walks to it, so there is a rule between the mechanism
-- and the kind that carries it.
mechanism_modelled(label_boundary).
mechanism_modelled(definition_time_call).
-- PER_CONSTRUCTION JOINED 2026-09-08 (w_class_fields), answered rather than
-- open: `field_init` reads the two kinds by mechanism and puts the initialiser
-- in `guard_arm`, so there is a rule between the mechanism and the kinds.
mechanism_modelled(per_construction).
-- ITERATOR_CALL JOINED 2026-09-08 (w_destructuring_hides_a_call), answered
-- rather than open: `pattern_iterates` reads the two pattern kinds by KIND and
-- `for_of_iterates` reads the loop, so every kind carrying it has a rule.
mechanism_modelled(iterator_call).
edb(mechanism_waived).      -- mechanism_waived(M, Reason)
mechanism_waived(loop_at_least_once, a_body_that_always_runs_guards_nothing).

-- THREE STATES, NOT TWO. The first draft had modelled and waived, and `abrupt`
-- was neither — so `mechanism_unanswered[audit]` stood at one, permanently, for
-- a mechanism nobody had forgotten. AN AUDIT THAT IS RED ON AN HONEST TREE GETS
-- SWITCHED OFF, and this repository has paid for that once already. Declaring
-- the openness with its OWNER keeps the row queryable and the audit at zero,
-- which is the same move `cell_blocked` makes for a cell nobody can take.
edb(mechanism_open).        -- mechanism_open(M, WorkItem)
-- EMPTY SINCE 2026-09-06, and kept declared: `abrupt` was the only row and it
-- moved to `mechanism_modelled` when w_cf_abrupt_transfer landed. The relation
-- stays because the THREE-STATE shape is the point — the next mechanism that is
-- neither answered nor waived needs somewhere honest to sit, and re-deriving
-- that lesson costs more than one `edb` line.

mechanism_known(M) :- mechanism_modelled(M).
mechanism_known(M) :- mechanism_waived(M, _).
mechanism_known(M) :- mechanism_open(M, _).

-- A MECHANISM WITH NO OPINION AT ALL: not modelled, not waived, not declared
-- open. A kind carrying one is silently read as unconditional — the WRONG
-- direction, since it makes the guarded set too small and `may_not_run` too
-- narrow, and a may-set that is too narrow is a claim rather than a hedge.
mechanism_unanswered[audit](M) :- transfer_mechanism(_, M), not mechanism_known(M).

-- ...and the same question one level down: a kind carrying a MODELLED mechanism
-- that no rule of this layer actually reaches.
guard_named[code](K)       :- guard_kind(K, _).
guard_named[code](K)       :- short_circuit_kind(K).
-- ...and the four abrupt kinds, reached by `abrupt_at` rather than by an arm.
guard_named[code](K)       :- abrupt_kind(K).
-- ...and the two SUSPENSION kinds, reached by `suspend_at` on the same pattern.
-- DERIVED FROM `transfer_mechanism` rather than listed, which is what caught
-- the incomplete change: declaring `mechanism_modelled(suspend)` without this
-- line put both kinds in `guard_unmodelled[audit]` within one run, and that is
-- the audit doing exactly what it was written for — a mechanism claimed as
-- answered by a rule nothing connects to the kinds that carry it.
guard_named[code](K)       :- transfer_mechanism(K, suspend).
-- ...and the LABEL, on the same pattern and for the same reason: derived from
-- the mechanism, so declaring `mechanism_modelled(label_boundary)` without a
-- rule that reaches `labeled_statement` reddens `guard_unmodelled[audit]`
-- instead of passing quietly.
guard_named[code](K)       :- transfer_mechanism(K, label_boundary).
-- ...and the two FIELD kinds, on the same pattern: derived from the mechanism,
-- so declaring `mechanism_modelled(per_construction)` without `field_init`
-- reaching them reddens `guard_unmodelled[audit]` instead of passing quietly.
guard_named[code](K)       :- transfer_mechanism(K, per_construction).
-- `catch_clause` carries `skip_arm` and is reached as the ARM of a
-- `try_statement` rather than by a `guard_kind` row of its own — the guard is
-- the try's `handler` field, and the clause is what sits in it. Named here so
-- the closed-vocabulary gate does not report a kind the rules really do cover.
guard_named[code](catch_clause).
-- ...and the same for the switch: `switch_statement` transfers control by
-- selecting a case, and the arm that may be skipped belongs to `switch_case`.
guard_named[code](switch_statement).
-- ...and `member_expression`, which carries `accessor_call` and is reached by
-- `accessor_read` rather than by a `guard_kind` row: an accessor read transfers
-- control INTO a getter, it does not skip an arm. Named here for the same
-- reason `catch_clause` is — so the closed-vocabulary gate does not report a
-- kind the rules really do cover.
guard_named[code](member_expression).
-- ...and `decorator`, for exactly the reason above it: a decorator transfers
-- control INTO the decorator function, it does not skip an arm, so it is
-- reached through the call graph rather than by a `guard_kind` row.
guard_named[code](decorator).
-- ...and the THREE KINDS THAT READ AN OBJECT'S KEYS, 2026-09-08. Each is
-- reached by `pattern_accessor` rather than by a `guard_kind` row, for the same
-- reason `member_expression` is: a hidden getter call transfers control INTO the
-- getter and skips no arm. Written out one per kind rather than derived from
-- `transfer_mechanism(K, accessor_call)`, because a derived line would also
-- cover `member_expression` and silence the mutant that guards the row above.
guard_named[code](object_pattern).
guard_named[code](rest_element).
guard_named[code](spread_element).
-- ...and the ITERABLE side, derived from the mechanism on the pattern
-- `label_boundary` and `per_construction` set: declaring
-- `mechanism_modelled(iterator_call)` without a rule that reaches
-- `array_pattern`, `spread_element` and `for_of_statement` reddens
-- `guard_unmodelled[audit]` instead of passing quietly.
guard_named[code](K)       :- transfer_mechanism(K, iterator_call).
guard_unmodelled[audit](K) :- transfer_mechanism(K, M), mechanism_modelled(M),
                              ast_node[code](_, K, _, _), not guard_named[code](K).

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- SHORT-CIRCUIT. `a?.b()` does not skip a CHILD — it stops the whole chain when
-- the object is nullish, so the site itself is conditional, and so is anything
-- further along the same member chain.
edb(short_circuit_kind).
short_circuit_kind(optional_call_expression).
short_circuit_kind(optional_member_expression).

guarded[code](N) :- short_circuit_kind(K), ast_node[code](N, K, _, _).
guarded[code](N) :- short_circuit_kind(K), ast_node[code](P, K, _, _),
                    ast_within[code](P, N).

-- AN ARM DECLARED FOR A FIELD THE SCANNER NEVER EMITS UNDER THAT KIND — a typo
-- in `guard_kind` is otherwise invisible: the row simply matches nothing and
-- the guarded set is quietly smaller.
guard_arm_seen[code](K, Field) :- guard_kind(K, Field), ast_node[code](P, K, _, _),
                                  ast_child[code](P, Field, 0, _).
guard_arm_unseen[audit](K, F)  :- guard_kind(K, F), ast_node[code](_, K, _, _),
                                  not guard_arm_seen[code](K, F).

-- A GUARDED SITE AT A COORDINATE, so the report can point at the source and the
-- acceptance can join it against what the runtime did.
guarded_at[code](File, Line) :- guarded_call[code](C), ast_node[code](C, _, File, Line).
